# 40. Central Conclusion

The American conservation system should not ultimately be evaluated by the size of its bureaucracy, the number of programs it contains, the amount of money it spends, or the number of projects it completes.

Those are inputs and activities.

The more consequential question is whether the system can repeatedly perform the following sequence:

> **Understand the ecological condition → identify the limiting problem → assign authority → provide resources → build capacity → coordinate action → implement an intervention → measure the response → learn from the evidence → adapt management → maintain the result.**

The conservation system succeeds when that cycle works.

It struggles when one of those links repeatedly breaks.

That provides the conceptual bridge from the individual policy pillars to the **American Conservation Renewal framework itself**.
